# The loop guard, second batch: the cases that take goal_met and repeats_check to 50 (74 cases).
#
# This file holds ONLY the new cases. The original 26 stay in `loop.jsonl` so that the run of
# 2026-09-24 and its recording remain reproducible exactly as published. Together the two
# files give 50 goal_met and 50 repeats_check.
#
# Written to be harder than the first batch on purpose, because the first batch scored 14/14
# and 12/12 and a bench that nothing fails measures nothing. The difficulty is concentrated in
# four families, all of which are real failure modes rather than word games:
#
#  A. Partial completion that reads as complete. A goal with two or three parts where the
#     `done` answers the loudest part fully and the quiet one not at all.
#  B. Effort narrated as result. A `done` that describes searching, reading or running, and
#     never states an outcome. The giveaway is that no sentence in it is a fact about the
#     world.
#  C. Corroboration mistaken for repetition. The same fact checked against a *different*
#     source is new evidence, not a repeat; the same fact checked against the same source is.
#  D. Staleness. An identical-looking check is not a repeat when something changed between
#     the first check and now. The change may be in the artefact, in the inputs, or in scope.
#
# Two cases are declared negative controls before the run, on the criterion of paper Section
# 5.3: gm-44 and rp-46 turn on comparing dates or counting, which this model class reads as
# text. They are expected to fail and are not evidence of anything if they do.
#
# Annotator 1 (author) labelled every case before any decision was run. Annotator 2 is a
# separate generative model, prompted from the point's own criteria and blind to both this
# label and the evaluator's answer; agreement is reported in the results directory. That is
# not two independent humans, and the paper says so where it uses these numbers.
